
  - [1mMy (a) "training-prep keeps authoring the initial range" was wrong[0m — it doesn't author anything. The question is really about the microcycle
  note.
  - [1mYou asked whether per-mesocycle range changes would play well with this.[0m They're already how it works. [38;5;153m15–20[39m isn't stale data — it's your
  [3mcurrent[0m stated intent for this block. A retune to [38;5;153m8–12[39m in the store would silently contradict the note you're still maintaining, and next
  mesocycle you'd write [38;5;153m15–20[39m again and it would never take effect. That's the mirror image of today's bug: instead of a range nothing can
  change, a range your authoring can't change.

  [1mRevised options — who owns the range:[0m

  - [1m(a) Store wins; retire the microcycle's rep-range column.[0m The retune owns it outright, the agent stops sending. Clean single owner, but you
  lose the ability to [3mdeliberately[0m set a range per mesocycle — the thing you told me you want.
  - [1m(b) Microcycle authors, store adapts within the block.[0m [3m(Recommended.)[0m A posted range that [1mdiffers from the last one posted[0m for that exercise
  is a deliberate re-authoring and overwrites the stored range; a posted range [1midentical[0m to the last is a no-op, so the nightly repost never
  clobbers a retune. New exercises get their range on first sight for free. You keep per-mesocycle intent, the retune adapts inside it, and
  neither silently beats the other.
  - [1m(c) The retune writes back into the microcycle note.[0m Truest to "one source", but it means strength-log editing a vault file the training
  agent owns — a new cross-repo write path, and ADR 0039 spent real effort keeping that surface small.

  I'd take [1m(b)[0m. It's a change to ticket 02's scope — from "stop sending" to "adopt on change" — and it's implementable entirely inside
  strength-log, so [38;5;153mpersonal-agents[39m needs no change at all.

  Worth noting under (b): accepting a retune to [38;5;153m8–12[39m while the note still says [38;5;153m15–20[39m leaves them disagreeing until you next edit the microcycle.
  That's tolerable — the note is intent, the store is what the app runs on — but if that bothers you, (c) is the only option that fixes it.

  Slice 03 is unaffected and still running.

